-- title:
--   The mod-p form condition is K-linear
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and $m$ a natural number, and write $\bar\jmath =$ `jqModC K` for the Laurent series $q^{-1}\cdot(\text{image in }K[[q]]\text{ of the integral power series } \mathtt{jNum})$, i.e. the reduction to $K$ of the $q$-expansion of $j$. For $G \in K((q)) =$ `LaurentSeries K`, the predicate `IsModPFormFn K m G` asserts two integrality conditions: first, that $G^{6}\,\bar\jmath^{\,4m}\,(\bar\jmath - 1728)^{3m}$ is integral over the $K$-subalgebra of $K((q))$ generated by $\bar\jmath$, and second, that $G^{2}\,\bar\jmath^{\,m}\,(\bar\jmath - 1728)^{m}$ is integral over the $K$-subalgebra generated by $\bar\jmath^{-1}$ (here $1728$ denotes the image of $1728 \in K$ under the structure map $K \to K((q))$). The theorem asserts the conjunction of three statements: `IsModPFormFn K m 0` holds; for all $G, H \in K((q))$ satisfying `IsModPFormFn K m`, so does $G + H$; and for every $c \in K$ and every $G$ satisfying `IsModPFormFn K m`, so does $c \bullet G$. Thus the set of $G$ with `IsModPFormFn K m G` is a $K$-linear subspace of $K((q))$, with no hypothesis on the characteristic of $K$ and none of the form $1728 \ne 0$.
--
--   This is the closure statement making the set of $q$-expansions satisfying the integrality shape defining weight-$2m$ mod-$p$ modular functions a $K$-subspace of $K((q))$, so that dimension counts and linear-independence arguments may be carried out in it. It is used by the constructions of elements of the space of mod-$p$ forms from points of the modular curve and by the dimension estimate [`ModularCurve.exists_linearIndependent_isModPFormFn_rat_dimFormula_le_card`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_linearIndependent_isModPFormFn_rat_dimFormula_le_card).
